const COURSE_20_QUIZZES_DEF = {
  c20q_l1:{ title:'Quiz: The cloud is not magic', qs:[
    { q:'What is the cloud from a network design view?',
      opts:['a special environment where the routing and firewall rules of a physical network stop applying','someone else’s datacenter with your virtual network – just another location behind a router','a provider’s separate internet, reachable only through their own dedicated application'],
      correct:1, why:'The same rules apply as in any network: for traffic to reach a server, a path must exist and someone must permit it. Nothing flows there by magic.' },
    { q:'How is security responsibility split in the cloud?',
      opts:['the provider is responsible for absolutely everything, since servers and data run in their datacenter','the provider watches hardware and the datacenter, you watch your data, accounts and network rules','responsibility falls on whoever logged into the system last before a given incident occurred'],
      correct:1, why:'This is called shared responsibility. You answer for your data and who you expose it to – the provider gives a vault, but an open door is your mistake.' },
    { q:'Why is a publicly open bucket dangerous?',
      opts:['it slows other cloud services, because public access takes up more of the link’s capacity','it is an open zone: anyone on the internet who knows or guesses the address reaches the content','it automatically deletes older files to make room, so no evidence remains after a leak'],
      correct:1, why:'Switching storage to public is one checkbox and nothing glows red. Leave the default private and ask: who exactly should reach this and why?' }
  ]},
  c20q_l2:{ title:'Quiz: Site-to-site VPN', qs:[
    { q:'What is a site-to-site VPN into the cloud?',
      opts:['a temporary link of one laptop to the cloud, arising only for the duration of a user’s login','a permanent encrypted tunnel between the whole company network and the whole cloud network, between two boxes','a public address of a cloud server, letting anyone connect to it from the internet without verification'],
      correct:1, why:'It joins network to network and holds permanently between the firewall and the cloud gateway. Traffic goes over the internet but wrapped and encrypted, unreadable on the way.' },
    { q:'What should flow through the tunnel between company and cloud?',
      opts:['all traffic from both sides, since once the tunnel stands there is no reason to restrict it further with any rules on either end','only work-necessary traffic; a tunnel is a path, not a permission, so least privilege applies as between segments','solely traffic from servers, never from user computers, which have nothing to send to the cloud'],
      correct:1, why:'That a connection exists does not mean everyone should reach everyone through it. And a public website need not go through the cloud – split tunneling sends it out directly.' },
    { q:'Why is one tunnel into the cloud a single point of failure?',
      opts:['because after a few hours of continuous operation the tunnel always ends itself and must be renewed by hand','because it is the only path to the private servers; when it drops, the database is unreachable and apps stop','because the encryption in the tunnel wears out and over time stops reliably protecting traffic from eavesdropping'],
      correct:1, why:'You rightly did not expose the private servers to the internet, so there is no other path to them. It is solved by doubling: a second tunnel over another link or gateway.' },
    { q:'What does split tunneling mean?',
      opts:['the tunnel splits into two halves over different links, so transferred data moves faster','only cloud addresses are sent into the cloud, while ordinary internet traffic goes directly over the company link','traffic is split between two different cloud providers, so neither sees the whole content of the communication'],
      correct:1, why:'A public website has no reason to travel into the cloud first and out from there. The opposite – driving all through one end – is done deliberately for inspection, but costs performance.' }
  ]},
  c20q_l3:{ title:'Quiz: Remote access into the cloud', qs:[
    { q:'How does a remote-access VPN differ from site-to-site?',
      opts:['remote-access encrypts more strongly than site-to-site, since it expects connections from untrusted networks','at one end is a single user and the tunnel arises for the session; site-to-site joins networks permanently','remote-access works only inside the company building, whereas site-to-site links branches between cities'],
      correct:1, why:'A simple distinction: site-to-site joins places, remote-access joins people. So remote-access deals with strong verification of each person and their privileges.' },
    { q:'Which point of the cloud must be visible from the internet for a remote user to log in?',
      opts:['the database server, because the VPN client verifies the user account with it before opening the tunnel','the cloud gateway’s public address; it serves for login, the servers themselves have no public address','all servers in the private subnet at once, otherwise the user would have nothing to connect to after login'],
      correct:1, why:'Until the tunnel opens, nothing but the login gets through the gateway. Servers stay hidden – reached only through the tunnel, after proving identity.' },
    { q:'Why is "a VPN by itself not security"?',
      opts:['because a VPN is fairly easy to break, so an attacker eventually reads the tunnel content on the way anyway','because it handles only secure entry; if a user may reach everything after login, you only extended the attacker’s cable','because a VPN works only on computers, not on the phones and tablets that nowadays also connect to the cloud just like computers do'],
      correct:1, why:'The tunnel handles entry, not what you may do inside. A remote user should have privileges as narrow as in the office – the connection must be followed by a rule saying where.' },
    { q:'Which risk is the main one specifically for remote-access VPN?',
      opts:['that two networks after joining fall into the same address range and their traffic then starts mixing together uncontrollably','weak verification of people and too broad privileges after login: a weak password, a missing second factor, unrevoked access','that the tunnel carries too much data at once and thereby saturates the single internet link into the cloud'],
      correct:1, why:'Remote-access handles dozens of individuals connecting and disconnecting. So it watches strong verification of each, removal of access on departure and narrow privileges.' }
  ]},
  c20q_l4:{ title:'Quiz: Publishing a service', qs:[
    { q:'What does DNAT do when publishing a web from the cloud?',
      opts:['it encrypts the connection between visitor and server, so nobody reads it on the way over the internet','at the cloud edge it rewrites a packet aimed at the public address to the server’s internal address, and back','it distributes incoming requests among several identical servers, so none is overloaded by visitors'],
      correct:1, why:'The server has only an internal address; the service gets a public IP and DNAT does the translation. The visitor never knows the internal address, the server never the public – each talks to its side.' },
    { q:'What is a load balancer before a web farm for?',
      opts:['it encrypts traffic between the individual farm servers, so their mutual communication cannot be eavesdropped','it distributes requests among several identical servers and skips a fallen one – scaling and resilience at once','it stores copies of all data from the web servers, so they can be restored if a farm server fails'],
      correct:1, why:'The public IP belongs to the load balancer, not the individual servers. A health check watches which are alive, so a visitor does not notice one server’s failure.' },
    { q:'Which rule sums up the whole lesson on publishing?',
      opts:['expose only what truly must be public; management, the database and internal things stay hidden behind the tunnel','rather expose more services, so you can reach them quickly from anywhere without a lengthy login when needed','put everything with a public address on one server, so the whole public surface is neatly in one place'],
      correct:0, why:'A test for every port: must a stranger on the internet see this for the service to work? If not, it does not belong outside. The goal is the smallest public surface.' }
  ]},
  c20q_l5:{ title:'Quiz: A cloud DMZ and security groups', qs:[
    { q:'What is a security group in the cloud?',
      opts:['a group of users who share one login to the cloud and pass a single password between themselves','a rule set attached directly to a server saying who and to which port may connect – the cloud version of a zone firewall','a provider tariff that includes attack protection and must be paid for separately for each individual server and each open port'],
      correct:1, why:'A rule has the same parts as a firewall’s: from where, to which port, permit. And since it is attached to the server, it applies even to a neighbor in the same subnet – free segmentation.' },
    { q:'Where does the database belong when segmenting a cloud network?',
      opts:['in the public subnet next to the web, so web visitors have the fastest and shortest access to it','in a private subnet with no path from outside; it accepts only from the web servers on its port, and nobody else','on a completely separate server with its own public address, so admins reach it from anywhere on the internet'],
      correct:1, why:'A chain emerges: balancer → web → database, where each link talks only to its neighbor. When an attacker takes the web, at the database they hit a boundary – shrinking the blast radius.' },
    { q:'What is the most common mistake that buries the default deny in the cloud?',
      opts:['forgetting to add any rule at all, so by default not even the admin reaches the server','a rule permitting ‘from anywhere’ (0.0.0.0/0) to a management port, to ‘temporarily’ solve some problem','setting too many narrow rules at once, so the admin loses track and some of them contradict each other'],
      correct:1, why:'One broad permit lets the whole world in. The cure is the same trio as a firewall’s: a narrow source, a narrow port and a description – and walk the rules that permit from anywhere.' },
    { q:'How does the cloud differ from a physical network in the firewall’s default stance?',
      opts:['in the cloud rules are evaluated bottom-up, so neither order nor the existence of a default deny matters','a security group starts with a default deny, so a new server is closed until you explicitly permit access','in the cloud there is no firewall at all and the provider handles all traffic protection at the datacenter level'],
      correct:1, why:'That stance is set laboriously in a physical network; in the cloud you get it for free. So you cannot forget to close it – only open more than needed.' }
  ]},
  c20q_l6:{ title:'Quiz: Resilience and design', qs:[
    { q:'Why is one internet link a weak spot of cloud connectivity?',
      opts:['because one link never has enough capacity to handle all traffic into the cloud and back out of it at once during peak hours','because the whole connection to the cloud hangs on one cable: cut it and the company is cut off from its servers','because traffic over a single link cannot be encrypted, so the provider could easily read it on the way'],
      correct:1, why:'It does not matter how well the servers are designed inside. The answer is multi-WAN: a second link from another provider, routed physically differently, with a tested failover.' },
    { q:'Why is it not enough to trust that "data is in the cloud, so it is safe"?',
      opts:['because the cloud auto-deletes data after a year to free space, and you must download it by hand in time','because the provider will not protect you from your own mistake: a delete, a bad script or ransomware the cloud dutifully stores','because data in the cloud is slower to access than on a local disk, so it is harder to work with during an outage or a slowdown'],
      correct:1, why:'So a backup must be separate – another place and account, out of reach of everyday operations. And a backup you never tried to restore is not a backup but a hope.' },
    { q:'Which question best tests a cloud network design?',
      opts:['exactly how many servers and public addresses are deployed in the cloud and whether the count grew last year','if I captured the web server, where would I get from here? The ideal answer: to one database port and nothing more','how many different service providers the company uses and whether their agenda can be merged under one vendor'],
      correct:1, why:'It is the blast radius in the cloud: you will not stop the first breach, but you decide whether it becomes an incident on one server or the whole VPC. Then pick one weakness and fix it.' },
    { q:'Why are two regions considered for critical services?',
      opts:['because traffic between two regions is always cheaper than traffic within one region at the same provider','because a whole region can have an outage, and also where data sits, the local laws and rules apply','because each region can serve only a limited number of users and a second region simply doubles that ceiling'],
      correct:1, why:'Spreading across two regions keeps a critical service alive even during one region’s disaster. And for personal data the region choice is a decision, not a detail – it decides the applicable law.' }
  ]}
};
